4. Explain the steps how “Control Flow” controls the functions calls?

⚙️ What is Control Flow?

Control flow means the order in which code runs — how Node.js decides which function runs first, next, and last.

In Node.js, because it is asynchronous and non-blocking, control flow doesn’t always go line-by-line like normal code.
It depends on callbacks, promises, and the event loop.

🧩 Simple Steps — How Control Flow Works:

Let’s understand step by step 👇

🟢 Start the program
Node.js begins executing your file from top to bottom.

⚡ Synchronous code runs first
All normal (blocking) code runs line by line.

console.log("Start"); // runs first

🕒 Asynchronous functions are registered
Functions like setTimeout, fs.readFile, or API calls are sent to background to be handled later.
Node.js doesn’t wait for them — it moves on to the next line.

setTimeout(() => console.log("Async Task"), 1000);

➡️ Continue with next lines of code
Node.js executes the rest of the code while the async task is still running in the background.

console.log("End");

🔁 Event Loop checks for completed tasks
When the async task finishes, its callback (or promise) is sent back to the event loop, which puts it in the callback queue.

🎯 Execute callbacks one by one
When the main thread (call stack) is empty, the event loop picks the next callback and runs it.

💬 Example:
console.log("Start");

setTimeout(() => {
console.log("Async Task Done");
}, 1000);

console.log("End");

🧠 Output:

Start
End
Async Task Done

Because the control flow first runs all synchronous code, then later handles asynchronous callbacks.

🪄 In short:

Node.js control flow means:
1️⃣ Run synchronous code first →
2️⃣ Send async tasks to background →
3️⃣ When they finish, the event loop runs their callbacks.

