14. Suppose you have a server with many incoming requests, and users are hitting APIs. Do these APIs use the thread pool?

👉 When many users hit a server, it does NOT create one thread per request.
Instead, it uses a smart system from the OS (like epoll/kqueue) to handle everything efficiently.

🏠 Real-Life Example (Very Easy)

Imagine a restaurant 🍽️

❌ Old Method (Bad Way)
100 customers come
You assign 1 waiter per customer
So you need 100 waiters 😵

👉 Problem:

Too many waiters = expensive + slow + messy
✅ Smart Method (Used in Node.js / libuv)
You have just a few waiters (threads)
They don’t stand with one customer
Instead, they:
Take order from one table
Move to another table
Come back when food is ready

👉 This is how Node.js works

💻 Now Map This to Your Question
🔹 What are “requests”?
Users calling APIs (like opening Instagram, YouTube, etc.)
🔹 What is a “thread”?
A worker that handles tasks
❌ Does each API use a thread pool?

👉 NO

API requests (network calls) are handled differently
They don’t use thread pool
🤔 Then how are requests handled?

Instead of creating many threads, Node.js uses:

⚙️ OS Mechanisms (epoll / kqueue)

Think of them like:
👉 A smart watchman 👀 who watches all doors (connections)

🧩 How it works step-by-step
Many users send requests → come to server
Each request connects via a socket (like a communication pipe)
OS keeps track of all sockets using:
epoll (Linux)
kqueue (macOS)
When something happens (data arrives):
👉 OS says: “Hey! This socket is ready!”
Node.js handles it without creating new threads
🎯 Key Point

👉 Instead of:
❌ 1 thread per request

👉 We use:

✅ 1 system to monitor thousands of requests
🔥 Why is this powerful?
Handles thousands of users at once
Uses very few resources
Very fast and scalable
🧠 Final Simple Summary

👉 APIs (network requests)
➡️ Do NOT use thread pool

👉 Instead, they use:

Sockets
OS tools like epoll/kqueue

👉 Which helps:

Handle many users
Without creating many threads

